# Lint after every change

After you edit, add, or delete code, run lint on the files you touched before you finish. Treat lint errors **and** warnings as blockers. Do not leave either in the diff.

## How to run

Use npm (never pnpm or yarn). Prefer the workspace that owns the files:

```bash
npx eslint path/to/changed-file.tsx
# or
npm run lint --workspace=web
npm run lint --workspace=admin
npm run lint --workspace=@repo/ui
npm run lint --workspace=@repo/types
```

If several workspaces changed, lint each one. Root `npm run lint` is fine when the change is repo-wide.

## What to do with results

- Fix every error and warning you introduced, and any you can fix in files you already edited.
- Do not disable a rule (`eslint-disable`) unless the rule is wrong for that line and a short comment explains why.
- Do not ignore pre-existing lint in files you did not touch.
- If lint fails, fix it and re-run until it is clean. Do not declare the task done while lint is failing.

```tsx
// BAD — ship with a warning or skip lint
// eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps

// GOOD — run lint on the changed files, then fix the reported issue
```
